# gemma4-31b-dense-bf16-q8-df.recipe -- Gemma 4 31B dense (text) from Google's bf16 checkpoint at
# eight bits (Q8_0's grid as libr4d serves it), with z-lab's DFlash drafter at eight bits too.
#
#   rad-convert <gemma-4-31B-it> --draft-model <gemma-4-31B-it-DFlash> \
#       --recipe data/recipes/gemma4-31b-dense-bf16-q8-df.recipe \
#       --set quantization_config.quant_method=compressed-tensors -o gemma4-31b-dense-q8-df.rad
#
#   radiance serve gemma4-31b-dense-q8-df.rad --num-speculative-tokens auto ...
#
# Q8_0 is int8 codes, symmetric, one f16 scale per block, the scale amax / 127: exactly `codes=i8
# scale=f16 clamp=sym` and the default absmax rule. What differs is the block. GGML's is 32 values
# and libr4d's int8 GEMMs read 128 (their group is compiled in, C_EQ("group", 128)), so a block
# here is four of Q8_0's. Every rule below is that one grid:
#
#   the trunk's linears    gemm_nt_q at w8a8: the w4a8 grid at twice the radix, read by the same
#                          fused junctions (rmsnorm_quant_i8, qkv_norm_rope, gelu_mul_quant_i8), so
#                          the step takes the int4 container's launches with twice its weight bytes
#   the drafter's linears  gemm_nt_q at w8a8 too, rmsnorm_quant_i8 folding each residual add, norm
#                          and quantiser (arch/common/rad_gemma4_dflash.h)
#   the drafter's head     its own int8 copy of the tied table, gemm_nt_q at w8a8
#
# The activations are int8 with a scale per row, not per 32-block (llama.cpp's q8_1): the w8a8
# grid's quantiser is per row, the one the int4 container already reads. libr4d's other int8 grid,
# i8a8, takes a scale per (row, 128) and is closer still to Q8_0 x Q8_1, but nothing in libr4d
# fuses a norm into its quantiser yet, so it would cost five launches per junction at decode.
#
# The trunk's lm_head is int8 as well, as Q8_0 makes `output.weight`: logits_gemm_i8 reads it
# against the bf16 hidden state (no activation quantiser), with a bf16 scale per row and 128
# columns. It is the trunk's verifier, so drop the line to keep it bf16, at 352 MB per rank more
# read each step. The embedding table stays bf16: it is read one row per token, and it is the
# drafter's table too.
#
# ~33 GiB: the int8 trunk's linears (~28 GiB), the bf16 table and its int8 head (2 GiB), the
# vision tower, the drafter (1.4 GiB) and its head's copy (0.7 GiB). At tp 4, ~8 GiB per rank on a
# 32 GiB R9700.
#
# This starts from bf16 for the reason gemma4-31b-dense-bf16-w4a8-df.recipe gives: the drafter
# reads the trunk's hidden states and was trained beside the bf16 trunk. q, k and v are one linear
# (blk.*.attn_qkv), for the reason that recipe gives.

blk.*.attn_qkv.weight     rtn  codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
blk.*.attn_o.weight       rtn  codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
blk.*.ffn_down.weight     rtn  codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
blk.*.ffn_gate_up.weight  rtn  codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym

output.weight             rtn  codes=i8 group=128 scale=bf16 clamp=sym

dflash.fc.weight                 rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.blk.*.attn_q.weight       rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.blk.*.attn_k.weight       rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.blk.*.attn_v.weight       rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.blk.*.attn_output.weight  rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.blk.*.ffn_gate_up.weight  rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.blk.*.ffn_down.weight     rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
dflash.draft_head.weight         rtn codes=i8 group=128 scale=f16 clamp=sym
